A||||7||FIEROCIOUS||DXR_DAD I have a question! Or anyone else!||||5||
Z||000000||FIEROCIOUS||02-21-2000||01:10 AM||||DXR_DAD or anyone else who can answer.<P>    I've read posts on the mustang hood scoop, but no one has writen how they cut the hole, if they used the RMF shield, or if they cut the post in the center near the opening!<P>    I feel the RMF shield is useless. How does metal on the decklid protect the ECM from RMF if the ECM is already protected by the fire wall? I would think it has more to do with the antanea. <BR>DXR_DAD if you did keep the shield how did you cut the hole to keep it?<BR> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||DXR_DAD||02-21-2000||01:18 AM||||That info is all on DaRkLoRD's web site. <A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com." TARGET=_blank>www.fieroproject.com.</A> go to mods and then click on the Finale.<P>Yes I kept the RMF shield. From all the info I got, It was best to keep it and mount it on the underside of the scoop. <p>[This message has been edited by DXR_DAD (edited 02-21-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000002||DXR_DAD||02-21-2000||01:32 AM||||"HERE'S THE SCOOP"    Mounted on  86 SE V6 <BR> <BR> <BR>The scoop is 25" wide and 22" long.  The rear mounting studs are 20" apart, the front are <BR>23".  Here is were we get into a  little trouble. You must cut out all of the hump for the <BR>scoop to fit, (hump about 22" x 9-12") this is how much needs to be cut.  Brackets must <BR>be used to mount the rear studs.  you will see what you need once the cut is made.  The <BR>brackets have slotted holes for adjustment to center the scoop (1 1/4  on each side). The <BR>front mounting studs are easy,  just mark the holes after line-up is done drill holes and it <BR>should be that easy. <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR>From my info the EMI plate is needed, this is were the hump is, so remove the plate ( used <BR>Dremel tool to remove ). Cut at edge of support braces. Now mount the plate to the under <BR>side of scoop ( used form-a-gasket high temp. ) on the three strips on the plate, then duct <BR>tape.  <BR> <BR>Now it`s time to cut out the hump (my hands shaking) can't turn back now?? <BR>Cover engine compartment, there will be lots of dust ( I used a cut-off wheel on my drill ) <BR>to make the cuts. <BR>Cut from the under side of the hood first (22" and between supports), from the top side <BR>(yes you must cut from the top!!) the hump starts about 1/2" from rear edge  before the <BR>support on the under side. Part of the support must be cut from the top. The edges do not <BR>need to be filed or sanded if it is a good half way clean cut because the edges are covered <BR>with 1/4" ID fuel hose ( 5ft.) spilt the hose slip over edges, ( form-a-gasket ) this will look <BR>nice and stop water from running directly into engine on plenum. Used a piece of pipe <BR>insulation to block off scoop on wet days or to keep debris out when parked. Take the <BR>scoop off and to the paint shop it goes, when you get it back all you need to do is 4 nuts. <BR>You may find some other ways and materials to use on this installation but this is what I <BR>did and it came out real nice. It looks like it's at home on my deck lid.  <BR> <BR>My instructions are to let you know that this can be done!! <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR>DXR_DAD <BR> <BR>09/16/99 <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||FIEROCIOUS||02-21-2000||02:44 AM||||Thanks mucho! DXR_DAD!<BR>I have a 87 GT and expect it to be easier (No hump) Will I have to make the brackets since I don't have the hump? I likely only have to drill holes. Right? The front of the decklid is at a 45 degree angle do you place it right at the edge when it is flat? One more question: does the convex of the hood match the scoop?<P><BR>Thanks Mike||||reg||10
Z||000004||DXR_DAD||02-21-2000||02:59 AM||||Yes FIEROCIOUS, go for it!! you have all the info you need. Yes the GT looks much easier, with out the hump.  I hope my info and photos help out. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||FIEROCIOUS||02-21-2000||03:05 AM||||DXR_DAD Thanks again for the moral support and information. I'll do it tomarrow! I'll tell you how it went! Thanks once again!<P>Mike ||||reg||1
